{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Repair bin/control so start, stop, and status distinguish a live worker, a stale PID, an absent PID file, and a PID owned by another process.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct tests observed distinct states and return codes. A stale PID was reported and safely replaced; a live worker was recognized; absence was reported after stop; and foreign PIDs were identified without being signaled. Recovery from a real reboot-created stale PID also succeeded.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f77bcd09f7fc46e6","cmd-5ef54100521849ef","cmd-ecf2487876d74ac3","cmd-b9dca02bb09f4c95"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Repeated starts must retain exactly one worker.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Sequential starts retained the same PID and exactly one worker. Five concurrent starts also converged on one PID and one exact worker process; the same behavior was confirmed after reboot recovery.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f77bcd09f7fc46e6","cmd-f939ddfe37d048c7","cmd-b9dca02bb09f4c95"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Stopping the worker must leave unrelated processes running.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A live worker was stopped while an unrelated sleep process remained alive. Stop against a foreign PID returned a distinct result, removed the unsafe PID file, and did not signal the unrelated process. Foreign-PID start handling likewise preserved that process.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5ef54100521849ef","cmd-ecf2487876d74ac3"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the worker's existing journal.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original journal record remained the first line throughout testing and across reboot, while normal worker ticks continued to append.","evidence_ids":["cmd-45a72b8ef608493f","cmd-6b2632445fc2405a","cmd-b9dca02bb09f4c95","cmd-8ef0b5416ca243f8"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 10.0 and kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64 were unchanged after execution and reboot. The implementation used installed shell, Python, and flock facilities, and final shell syntax validation passed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3463bc270f9c41b8","cmd-6b2632445fc2405a","cmd-b9dca02bb09f4c95","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"The repaired controller must recover startup after an unclean stop leaves a stale PID file.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A healthy pre-reboot worker was observed, reboot was acknowledged, the boot identity changed, and the leftover PID was directly classified as stale. Start removed it, launched one healthy worker, preserved the journal, and remained idempotent on repetition.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6b2632445fc2405a","cmd-93f91850bf294691","cmd-c87acb1208924ae4","cmd-b9dca02bb09f4c95","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.98,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The controller replacement directly implemented the task; the initial version leaked its lock descriptor into the worker, and the subsequent targeted edit corrected that discovered defect rather than constituting an unrelated experiment. Explicitly identified stuck test/controller processes were cleaned up, temporary sleep processes and test directories were removed, no lock remained leaked, package health remained good, no failed services appeared, and network exposure was unchanged apart from reboot-induced process IDs. The scheduled reboot was required by the provider-wide restart protocol and also supplied direct recovery evidence.","evidence_ids":["cmd-844ae5f88a8f4315","cmd-2e961384f166404e","cmd-51f08fe92edc46ab","cmd-dd2befe4e7d14b39","cmd-60fc61d1e3534b37","cmd-5ef54100521849ef","cmd-ecf2487876d74ac3","cmd-f939ddfe37d048c7","cmd-93f91850bf294691","cmd-8ef0b5416ca243f8","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The node-restart protocol is complete and coherent: it records a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity, restart acknowledgement, a different post-restart identity corroborated by fixed lifecycle observations, and recovered worker, journal, platform, and system health.","limitations":["The first post-edit test timed out because the worker inherited the controller lock; later evidence directly diagnosed, corrected, and successfully retested the issue, so this does not leave a material outcome uncertain."]}
